  (cid,82,299,'gap_fill','[Final] The modals we use to talk about governance — the _____ _____ _____ (must/should/could/might) — _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ (must/themselves/reflect) the _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ (deeply/normative/character) of the _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ _____ (enterprise/itself).','must / should / could / might / must themselves reflect / deeply normative character / enterprise itself',NULL,'The modal verbs — must/should/could/might — in governance discourse carry deep normative weight and must themselves be understood as encoding the values and aspirations of the scholarly enterprise.','hard'),
  (cid,82,300,'production','[TOEFL Writing — Final] Capstone 300: Write a concluding paragraph on advanced modals and academic discourse.','',NULL,'The advanced modals of English — must, should, could, might, needn''t, be supposed to, be bound to — are among the most epistemically and normatively loaded grammatical resources available to academic writers. To deploy them well is to navigate simultaneously the terrain of what is believed to be true and the terrain of what is believed to be right. The governance reform scholar who writes that past failures must have resulted from identifiable causes is making a claim about epistemic certainty; the one who argues that future reformers mustn''t repeat those failures is making a normative commitment; and the one who observes that better-designed reforms might have achieved more durable outcomes is exploring the space of counterfactual possibilities that the historical record forecloses but the analytical imagination can still inhabit. Each modal encodes a distinct relationship between the speaker and the proposition — a relationship of certainty or uncertainty, of obligation or permission, of possibility or inevitability. Mastery of these distinctions is not merely a grammatical accomplishment; it is an epistemological one. The TOEFL candidate who commands this range of expression is demonstrating not just grammatical competence but the kind of nuanced, calibrated engagement with evidence and argument that academic discourse at the highest level requires — and that the best governance scholarship, whatever its other limitations, has always aspired to embody.','hard')

